Verify resumable Stop preserves an active AUTO program and resumes or
single-steps it.

This directory holds the shared configuration (test.ini, core_sim.hal,
tool.tbl), the G-code programs and test-ui.py, which runs one scenario per
LinuxCNC session (selected by RESUMABLE_STOP_SCENARIO). The runtests tests
are its subdirectories, which can run in parallel:

  g76-stop   G76 Stop and clearance, the G76 cut/retract handoff stress test,
             fperrev
  g76-step   G76 Single Step (fresh, after Stop, in flight), exit tapers, and
             the trapezoidal re-run of fperrev
  threading  G33, G33.1 and G95 Stop cases, commands queued behind them, and
             the trapezoidal re-run of g33-chain
  general    everything else, including the at-speed, dwell, orientation and
             homing scenarios and the trapezoidal re-runs of ordinary and
             single-step

Each group lists its scenarios in its "scenarios" file. Its test.sh runs them
through run-scenarios.sh, which starts each from a fresh LinuxCNC session in a
private copy of the configuration, so groups never share files LinuxCNC
writes. Its checkresult (check-result.sh) fails unless every listed scenario
reported success and the count matches the number given in checkresult.

This regression test exercises the task-level Stop operation directly through
the Python command API. It checks that an active feed move stops without
aborting queued AUTO state, that spindle and coolant outputs are disabled
while stopped, and that Resume restores them before continuing the same
program to completion. test.ini selects the jerk-limited trajectory planner
(PLANNER_TYPE = 1) for every scenario, although position-synchronized moves
(G33, G76, G33.1) always use the trapezoidal planner; the ordinary,
single-step, fperrev and g33-chain scenarios run a second time with the
default trapezoidal planner (PLANNER_TYPE = 0), which joins the end of a G33
to the following feed with a blend arc. Every run rejects zero-limit Ruckig
fallback errors, which the G95 scenario could provoke while spindle feedback
recovers after Resume. The dwell-atspeed scenario checks that a
spindle at-speed barrier armed before Stop still holds the next feed after
Resume. The homing scenario issues Stop during a queued G28.2
and checks that the homing cycle completes before the program holds in
STOPPED.
The homing-interrupted scenario ends a queued G28.2 early by Abort, E-stop,
machine off, a mode change, and Stop followed at once by Abort and AUTO (from
homing-long.ngc, whose 8 s final homing move leaves time for that), and
checks that a later program runs, that Stop is rejected for an MDI command
and that it still reaches STOPPED in a new AUTO program.
The speed-change-atspeed scenario checks that an S-word after two Stop and
Resume cycles still makes the next feed wait for spindle at-speed.
The queued-feed-atspeed scenario stops while a queued feed waits for spindle
at-speed and checks that the feed still waits for at-speed after Resume.
The g33-chain scenario stops during the first of two consecutive G33 moves
and checks that Stop lets both finish before STOPPED, and that after Resume
Z stays put and nothing moves faster than the following feed.
The step-resume-stopping scenario sends Step and then Resume while Stop waits
for G33 and checks that the program then runs on without single-stepping.
The g95-between-g33 scenario stops during a G95 move between two G33 moves
and checks that the second G33 waits for a fresh spindle index after Resume.
The command-after-sync scenario stops during a G33 followed by M5, an S-word
or M9, and during a rigid tap followed by M5, and checks that the command
takes effect after Resume, in program order.
The resume-atspeed scenario checks that Resume, and Single Step from STOPPED,
wait in STARTING until the restored spindle reports at-speed before motion
continues: not at all while spindle.0.at-speed has never read false with the
spindle on (tied true), until at-speed goes true when it was false while
stopped, and, when at-speed stayed true through the Stop, until it has
dropped after the restore and come back. It also checks that a second Stop
returns to STOPPED and that Abort ends the program while waiting. Since
Resume waits for at-speed, rapid-no-atspeed checks that an interrupted rapid
also waits and that the feed after it still waits for the program's M3
barrier, and queued-g33 drops and restores at-speed after Resume as a
spindle would while spinning up.
The pause-starting scenario pauses while Resume waits in STARTING and checks
that the program returns to STOPPED with the spindle off and stays there when
at-speed arrives, then resumes normally; pause-latched-resume pauses after a
Resume latched while Stop waits for G33 and checks that the program stays
STOPPED once G33 ends. The resume-s0 scenario checks that Resume does not
wait for at-speed for a spindle restored at S0.
The spindle-state scenario checks that an S-word for a spindle stopped by M5
leaves it stopped after a Stop and Resume of the other spindle.
The G76 scenarios check that Stop executes only the
explicitly marked post-cut rapid retract, leaving the tool clear of the
finished thread rather than stopped at cutting depth. Coverage includes
cylindrical and tapered drive lines, synchronized exit tapers, repeated Stop
requests across the cut/retract handoff, and a negative G33 test proving that
an unrelated following rapid is not executed. Single Step coverage enters the
first G76 pass directly and verifies every cylindrical and tapered safe
approach step followed by its combined infeed, synchronized-cut, and clearance
step. It also starts G76 in normal AUTO_RUN, stops after the first pass with
later passes already queued, and verifies that each following AUTO_STEP
reaches exactly one cylindrical or tapered safe approach endpoint, and the
next AUTO_STEP completes that pass's infeed, thread, and clearance.
In-flight Single Step coverage arms during an ordinary source block with three
later motion blocks already queued and during an active G76 cut with later
passes queued. It verifies that the active block or thread-plus-clearance unit
finishes normally and motion remains at its boundary until Resume. While the
step is armed and the active unit is still moving, both task and motion report
unpaused; they change to paused only when motion reaches the latched boundary.
Arc blending is enabled for all scenarios, so the exact-clearance assertions
also prove that a marked G76 retract endpoint is not trimmed by a following
rapid. The handoff stress case uses a slow synchronized feed and issues Stop at
least 20 task cycles before the cut endpoint.
